UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 27, Lesson 1: Classroom instruments', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Classroom instruments come in families. Percussion instruments are hit or shaken, such as drums, tambourines, claves and shakers. Tuned percussion, such as a xylophone or glockenspiel, has bars of different lengths: short bars sound higher and long bars sound lower. Wind instruments, such as the recorder, need air. Each instrument has a way to play it correctly: hold the mallet loosely, hit the centre of the bar and let it ring. Carry instruments carefully and keep them away from water. Check with an adult before using any instrument that needs a mouth.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort these into families and say which xylophone bar gives the lower sound: a tambourine, a glockenspiel, a drum and a recorder.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind instrument. The longer bar gives the lower sound, and the short bar gives the higher sound.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest. Gentle, accurate playing gives a clearer sound, and hitting hard can damage the instrument or hurt your hands.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFind three instruments or household objects that can make sounds. Sort them into families, play a pattern on each and describe the pitch and loudness. Draw each one and label how you play it correctly.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Classroom instruments come in families. Percussion instruments are hit or shaken, such as drums, tambourines, claves and shakers. Tuned percussion, such as a xylophone or glockenspiel, has bars of different lengths: short bars sound higher and long bars sound lower. Wind instruments, such as the recorder, need air. Each instrument has a way to play it correctly: hold the mallet loosely, hit the centre of the bar and let it ring. Carry instruments carefully and keep them away from water. Check with an adult before using any instrument that needs a mouth.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-MUS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Find three instruments or household objects that can make sounds. Sort them into families, play a pattern on each and describe the pitch and loudness. Draw each one and label how you play it correctly. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a drum on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.. Correction: Strong answer: tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest. Gentle, accurate playing gives a clearer sound, and hitting hard can damage the instrument or hurt your hands. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a drum on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a drum, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a drum with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a drum. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a drum. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind instrument.","Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson."],"question":"Sort these into families and say which xylophone bar gives the lower sound: a tambourine, a glockenspiel, a drum and a recorder. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a drum; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.","Classroom instruments come in families.","tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind instrument.","Careful use of a shaker and explain why it matters with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a drum?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a shaker and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.","Name a shaker and explain why it matters only, without linking it to a drum.","Apply a drum to new material and name the a shaker and explain why it matters used."],"question":"Which response best applies a drum with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on a drum. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.","tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind instrument.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for a drum?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for a drum.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a drum?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: Pat plays a xylophone.","Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Classroom instruments come in families. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for a drum?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.","Name a shaker and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Find three instruments or household objects that can make sounds.","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies a drum to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a drum with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a drum on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a drum on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a drum. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a drum, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest.. Correction: Strong answer: tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w27-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a drum.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use a drum with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a shaker and explain why it matters.","I can practise a xylophone independently on a new prompt.","I can notice an instrument family needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Classroom instruments come in families.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Classroom instruments come in families.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a drum with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Pat plays a xylophone. He strikes a short bar and hears a high pitch, then strikes a long bar and hears a lower pitch. He uses soft mallets and bounces them off the bar so the sound rings. He sorts his group''s instruments into families: drum and shaker (percussion), xylophone (tuned percussion).\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort these into families and say which xylophone bar gives the lower sound: a tambourine, a glockenspiel, a drum and a recorder.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind instrument. The longer bar gives the lower sound, and the short bar gives the higher sound.","explain":"Classroom instruments come in families. Percussion instruments are hit or shaken, such as drums, tambourines, claves and shakers. Tuned percussion, such as a xylophone or glockenspiel, has bars of different lengths: short bars sound higher and long bars sound lower. Wind instruments, such as the recorder, need air. Each instrument has a way to play it correctly: hold the mallet loosely, hit the centre of the bar and let it ring. Carry instruments carefully and keep them away from water. Check with an adult before using any instrument that needs a mouth.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest. Gentle, accurate playing gives a clearer sound, and hitting hard can damage the instrument or hurt your hands. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Instrument families.","caption":"Group by how they sound.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Percussion</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">drum, shaker, tambourine</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wind</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">recorder</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Pat plays a xylophone. He strikes a short bar and hears a high pitch, then strikes a long bar and hears a lower Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Pat plays a xylophone. He strikes a short bar and hears a high pitch, then strikes a long bar and hears a lower Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a drum with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Pat plays a xylophone. He strikes a short bar and hears a high pitch, then strikes a long bar and hears a lower pitch. He uses soft mallets and bounces them off the bar so the sound rings. He sorts his group''s instruments into families: drum and shaker (percussion), xylophone (tuned percussion). Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Xylophone bar length.","caption":"Short is high.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Short bar</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">higher sound</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Long bar</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lower sound</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a drum with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest. Gentle, accurate playing gives a clearer sound, and hitting hard can damage the instrument or hurt your hands. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Pat plays a xylophone. He strikes a short bar and hears a high pitch, then strikes a long bar and hears a lower pitch. He uses soft mallets and bounces them off the bar so the sound rings. He sorts his group''s instruments into families: drum and shaker (percussion), xylophone (tuned percussion).\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: tambourine and drum are percussion, the glockenspiel is tuned percussion and the recorder is a wind instrument. The longer bar gives the lower sound, and the short bar gives the higher sound.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that an instrument gives the best sound when you hit it hardest. Gentle, accurate playing gives a clearer sound, and hitting hard can damage the instrument or hurt your hands. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a drum with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nClassroom instruments come in families. Percussion instruments are hit or shaken, such as drums, tambourines, claves and shakers. Tuned percussion, such as a xylophone or glockenspiel, has bars of different lengths: short bars sound higher and long bars sound lower. Wind instruments, such as the recorder, need air. Each instrument has a way to play it correctly: hold the mallet loosely, hit the centre of the bar and let it ring. Carry instruments carefully and keep them away from water. Check with an adult before using any instrument that needs a mouth. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 27, Lesson 1: Classroom instruments","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Singing, instruments and composing","week":27,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Pat plays a xylophone. He strikes a short bar and hears a high pitch, then strikes a long bar and hears a lower pitch. He uses soft mallets and bounces them off the bar so the sound rings. He sorts his group''s instruments into families: drum and shaker (percussion), xylophone (tuned percussion).\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Instrument families.","caption":"Group by how they sound.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Percussion</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">drum, shaker, tambourine</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wind</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">recorder</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Xylophone bar length.","caption":"Short is high.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Short bar</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">higher sound</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Long bar</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lower sound</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w27-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
